Objective

Apply defensive security skills to detect and block malicious activity using a simulated SIEM environment — just like a SOC analyst would.

Step 1: SIEM Monitoring & Alert Review

I began by monitoring the SIEM simulation dashboard provided in the room. Alerts are generated when suspicious activity is detected across network and endpoint logs.

I reviewed recent alerts to identify any unusual or suspicious behavior.

I spotted a source IP that was flagged as generating potentially malicious traffic.

Step 2: Validate Suspicious Activity

Once a suspicious IP was identified, I investigated it to confirm whether the activity was truly harmful.

I checked the context of the alerts and confirmed the activity was high risk.

Based on this investigation, I determined the IP must be blocked to protect the network.

Step 3: Threat Containment

With confirmation that the IP was malicious:

I blocked the suspicious source IP at the firewall level to stop further connections.

This containment action successfully halted the malicious activity and helped secure the environment.

Conclusion & Outcome

By detecting and validating the malicious activity using the SIEM simulation and then blocking the offending IP, I effectively applied defensive security principles:

SIEM monitoring to detect threats.

Alert investigation to validate malicious behavior.

Threat containment by blocking the source.

This activity demonstrates foundational SOC analyst skills in proactive defense and incident response.

Tools & Skills Used

SIEM (Security Information and Event Management) – to monitor and correlate events.

Threat investigation & analysis – to verify malicious activity.

Firewall rule creation – to block malicious IP connections.
